"""Form 5 Literature in English: Literary Appreciation Fundamentals and Prescribed Poetry Anthology (12 lessons).

All illustrative passages and poems in these lessons are invented by KKEVO Academy for teaching. No prescribed text is
quoted or described: students must use their own copies of the prescribed texts.
"""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("literature-in-english", "form-5")

M1 = "Literary Appreciation Fundamentals"
M2 = "Prescribed Poetry Anthology"

LESSONS = []

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1. Literary genres
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Literary genres",
    title="Literary genres: prose, poetry and drama",
    slug="lit1-literary-genres",
    minutes=20,
    summary="Literature has three main genres, prose, poetry and drama, each with its own form and clues. This lesson shows how to recognise them and name common sub-genres such as the novel, the elegy and the tragedy.",
    objectives=[
        "Define genre and name the three main literary genres",
        "Recognise prose, poetry and drama from the way a passage is set out and written",
        "Name common sub-genres such as novel, short story, elegy, tragedy and comedy",
        "Avoid mixing up genre with subject or with the author's purpose",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Genre", "Prose", "Poetry", "Drama", "Fiction and non-fiction", "Sub-genre", "Stage directions"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("On an evening in the village, a grandmother tells a story about a clever tortoise. Later a young man sings a song about the rain. "
          "On Youth Day the school presents a short play. These are three different shapes of literature, and you have met all three before you opened a "
          "textbook. In this lesson we learn their names and how to tell them apart."),
        D("A **genre** is a kind or type of literature, grouped by its form and its way of presenting words. "
          "The three main genres are **prose**, **poetry** and **drama**.", title="Genre"),
        KEY("The three main genres", "Each genre has its own clues on the page.", cards=[
            ["Prose", "Ordinary writing in sentences and paragraphs. Novels and short stories are prose."],
            ["Poetry", "Words arranged in lines and stanzas, with close attention to sound, rhythm and images."],
            ["Drama", "A story told through speech and action, written to be acted on a stage. It has speakers' names and stage directions."],
        ]),
        IMG("f5lit-1-genre-tree.svg", alt="A tree diagram: Literature splits into Prose, Poetry and Drama, with examples under each such as novel, short story, elegy, sonnet, tragedy and comedy",
            caption="Figure 1. The three main genres and some sub-genres."),
        H("Fiction and non-fiction"),
        T("Another useful split is between **fiction**, which is invented (a made-up story), and **non-fiction**, which deals with real events and people "
          "(a biography or a speech). Novels, short stories, most poems and plays are fiction in this sense. Your prescribed texts are all works of imagination."),
        D("**Prose** is writing that runs on in sentences and paragraphs. **Poetry** is writing arranged in lines (often grouped in stanzas) and shaped by rhythm, "
          "sound and imagery. **Drama** is writing meant to be performed: it is told through dialogue, action and stage directions.", title="Prose, poetry, drama"),
        H("Sub-genres"),
        T("Each main genre has smaller kinds, called **sub-genres**."),
        KEY("Common sub-genres", "You should be able to name and describe these.", cards=[
            ["Prose", "A **novel** is a long story with many events and characters. A **short story** is brief and can be read in one sitting. A **folktale** is a traditional story passed on by word of mouth."],
            ["Poetry", "A **lyric** expresses a speaker's feelings. A **ballad** tells a story in verse. An **elegy** mourns someone who has died. A **sonnet** has fourteen lines. An **ode** praises its subject."],
            ["Drama", "A **tragedy** ends in disaster for the main character. A **comedy** ends happily, usually with problems solved and often with marriage or reunion."],
        ]),
        T("The Board's report names the four sections of Paper 2 as Drama (Shakespeare), African literature (a novel), Poetry (an anthology) and Prose (a non-African novel). "
          "So your prescribed set covers all three genres. Macbeth is a tragedy and As You Like It is a comedy. Check with your teacher which plays and novels your own class studies."),
        H("Spotting the genre"),
        EX("Worked example 1: name the genre", intro="These three passages are invented for practice. Name the genre of each. (a) 'On Monday the whole school gathered under the mango tree, and the principal began to speak.' "
           "(b) 'The river bends twice below the village / and twice it forgets its name.' "
           "(c) 'NGWA: (entering, out of breath) Where is my father? / MAMA: (without looking up) At the farm. Sit.'", steps=[
            S("Look at the layout", text="(a) runs on as a sentence like a paragraph. (b) is split into short lines with a slash showing the line break. (c) has speakers' names and a bracket telling the actor what to do."),
            S("Match the clues", text="Sentences and paragraphs mean prose. Short lines with imagery (a river that 'forgets') mean poetry. Speakers' names, dialogue and stage directions mean drama."),
            S("Answer", text="(a) prose, (b) poetry, (c) drama."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: name the sub-genre", intro="Name the best sub-genre. (a) A long story that follows a family through many chapters. (b) A poem written after the death of a village elder, mourning him. (c) A play in which a proud chief loses everything and dies at the end.", steps=[
            S("(a)", text="Long, many chapters, many events: a novel (prose)."),
            S("(b)", text="A poem of mourning is an elegy (poetry)."),
            S("(c)", text="A play with disaster for the main character is a tragedy (drama)."),
            S("Answer", text="(a) novel, (b) elegy, (c) tragedy."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Thinking poetry must rhyme", "Many fine poems, including modern African poems, do not rhyme at all. Poetry is recognised by lines, rhythm, compressed language and imagery, not only by rhyme."),
        MISTAKE("Treating a play like a novel", "In a play there is no narrator describing events. Write 'the character says' or 'the stage directions show', not 'the narrator explains'. In an extract question, say which speaker says what."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up genre and subject", "Genre is the form (a poem, a play). Subject or theme is what it is about (love, power, hunger). A poem about a harvest is still poetry; a play about a farm is still drama."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 1 (multiple choice) often gives a short passage and asks you to name its genre or sub-genre, or asks which feature belongs to which genre. In Paper 2 you must name and use the right terms for your set text (for example 'stanza' for a poem, 'scene' for a play).", title="Exam link"),
        QUIZ("Which feature is a clear sign of DRAMA?",
             ["Stanzas with a rhyme scheme", "Chapters narrated in the first person", "Speakers' names, dialogue and stage directions", "A title and an author's name"], 2,
             "A play is written to be performed, so it is set out as speech by named characters with directions for action.",
             hint="Think about what an actor needs on the page.",
             feedback=["Stanzas and rhyme point to poetry.", "Chapters and a narrator point to prose.", "", "Every genre has a title and author, so this does not separate drama from the others."]),
        QUIZ("An 'elegy' is best described as:",
             ["A play that ends happily", "A poem that mourns someone who has died", "A short story read in one sitting", "A poem of fourteen lines"], 1,
             "An elegy is a poem of mourning or sad remembrance.",
             hint="The word is linked to sadness and loss.",
             feedback=["That describes a comedy.", "", "That describes a short story.", "That describes a sonnet."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Name the three main literary genres and give one sub-genre of each.",
           "Prose (novel or short story), poetry (elegy, sonnet, ballad or lyric), drama (tragedy or comedy)."),
        PQ("Explain the difference between fiction and non-fiction. Give one example of each.",
           "Fiction is invented, such as a novel or a play. Non-fiction deals with real events and people, such as a biography or a news report."),
        PQ("A student writes 'the narrator tells us that Macbeth is afraid' while discussing a scene from a play. What is wrong with this sentence, and how can it be fixed?",
           "A play has no narrator. The student should write that the character shows or says he is afraid, or that the stage directions or speech reveal it."),
        SUM("A genre is a kind of literature. The main genres are prose, poetry and drama.",
            "Prose runs in sentences and paragraphs; poetry uses lines, stanzas, rhythm and imagery; drama uses dialogue and stage directions.",
            "Sub-genres include the novel, short story, elegy, sonnet, ballad, tragedy and comedy.",
            "Genre is the form of a work, not its subject. Poetry need not rhyme.",
            "Use the right terms for the genre in the exam: stanza for poetry, scene for drama, chapter for a novel."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2. Plot structure
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Plot structure",
    title="Plot structure: from exposition to resolution",
    slug="lit1-plot-structure",
    minutes=22,
    summary="The plot is the chain of events in a story, linked by cause and effect. This lesson names the stages (exposition, rising action, climax, falling action, resolution) and shows how conflict, suspense and flashback work.",
    objectives=[
        "Define plot and distinguish it from a simple list of events",
        "Name the five stages of a typical plot in order",
        "Explain conflict, suspense, flashback and subplot",
        "Place events from a short story on the plot structure",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Plot", "Exposition", "Rising action", "Climax", "Falling action", "Resolution", "Conflict", "Suspense", "Flashback", "Subplot"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Imagine a farmer who loses a goat. He searches the bush, asks neighbours, suspects a rival, and at last finds the goat in an unexpected place. "
          "If we simply say 'the goat was lost, then it was found', we have only a list. A **plot** explains why each thing happens and how one event leads to the next."),
        D("The **plot** is the planned sequence of events in a story, in which each event causes or leads to the next. "
          "A plot is more than a list of things that happen: it shows cause and effect.", title="Plot"),
        T("Here is an example of the difference. 'The chief died, and then the people mourned' is a list. 'The chief died, and the people mourned because they had lost their protector' "
          "shows cause. Plots work by cause and effect."),
        H("The stages of a plot"),
        T("Many stories follow a shape like a hill. The tension goes up to a high point and then comes down. The stages have names."),
        IMG("f5lit-1-plot-arc.svg", alt="A rising-and-falling line labelled exposition, rising action, climax, falling action and resolution",
            caption="Figure 1. The plot arc. Not every story follows it exactly."),
        KEY("The five stages", "Learn them in order.", cards=[
            ["Exposition", "The opening. We meet the characters, the place and the situation."],
            ["Rising action", "A problem begins and grows. Complications make it harder to solve."],
            ["Climax", "The turning point and moment of highest tension, when the main conflict meets its crisis."],
            ["Falling action", "The results of the climax unfold and tension eases."],
            ["Resolution", "The ending (also called the denouement): the problem is settled and we see where things stand."],
        ]),
        D("**Conflict** is the struggle at the centre of a story. It can be between people, between a person and society, between a person and nature, or inside a character's own mind.", title="Conflict"),
        D("**Suspense** is the feeling of anxious waiting that makes us want to know what will happen next. A **flashback** is a scene from the past inserted into the present story. "
          "A **subplot** is a smaller, secondary story running beside the main plot.", title="Suspense, flashback and subplot"),
        EX("Worked example 1: place the events", intro="An invented story: 'The Lost Goat'. (1) Ayuk, a farmer in a hill village, notices his best goat is missing. (2) He searches the bush and begins to suspect his neighbour Bih. (3) After a loud quarrel at Bih's gate, Ayuk finds the goat tied there, and the goat is in fact Bih's own, bought at market, which looks the same. (4) Ayuk apologises and the two men talk about the mistake. (5) Next morning Ayuk's goat walks home by itself, and the two men laugh and share a meal. Place each event on the plot arc.", steps=[
            S("Find the opening", text="Event 1 introduces the farmer and the problem: exposition."),
            S("Find the growth of the problem", text="Event 2 makes things harder (suspicion of a neighbour): rising action."),
            S("Find the peak", text="Event 3 is the quarrel and the discovery, the highest tension and the turning point: climax."),
            S("Find the easing", text="Event 4 shows the results of the climax (apology and talk): falling action."),
            S("Find the ending", text="Event 5 settles everything: resolution."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: cause and effect", intro="In 'The Lost Goat', explain how event 2 causes event 3.", steps=[
            S("State the first event", text="Ayuk, unable to find his goat, suspects Bih."),
            S("Link it to the next", text="Because he suspects Bih, he goes to Bih's gate and quarrels."),
            S("Explain the result", text="The quarrel leads to the discovery that makes the climax. Each event comes from the one before it."),
        ]),
        TIP("When asked about the 'climax', do not just say 'the most exciting part'. Name the event and say why it is the turning point: what changes after it?"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Retelling the whole story", "A question on plot usually asks about one part, such as how a conflict develops or what the climax is. Answer that part with events from the text, in order. The Board's report says that candidates struggle when they cannot tell where an episode starts and ends."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up events", "The Board's report notes that candidates sometimes write about one episode when the question asks about another. Read each part of the question twice and give events that belong to the right part."),
        MISTAKE("Assuming the climax is the last event", "The climax is the turning point, not the end. After it come the falling action and the resolution."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 1 (multiple choice) tests terms such as exposition, climax and resolution on a short outline of events. In Paper 2 you must describe events from your own set text accurately and in order, so make a short plot outline of each prescribed text from your own copy.", title="Exam link"),
        QUIZ("The 'climax' of a plot is:",
             ["The opening scene that introduces the characters", "The turning point of highest tension", "The final event that settles everything", "The part where the tension slowly eases"], 1,
             "The climax is the high point, where the conflict reaches a crisis and things turn.",
             hint="Look at the top of the arc in Figure 1.",
             feedback=["That is the exposition.", "", "That is the resolution.", "That is the falling action."]),
        QUIZ("In a story, a scene that goes back to something that happened ten years earlier is called a:",
             ["Subplot", "Flashback", "Climax", "Exposition"], 1,
             "A flashback takes us back in time to explain or add to the present.",
             hint="It 'flashes' back in time.",
             feedback=["A subplot is a smaller second story.", "", "The climax is the turning point.", "Exposition is the opening that sets the scene."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Name the five stages of a typical plot in order.", "Exposition, rising action, climax, falling action, resolution."),
        PQ("Give one example of each kind of conflict: person against person, person against society, person against nature, and inside a character.",
           "For example: two brothers quarrel over land (person against person); a girl wants school but her community will not allow it (person against society); a fisherman fights a storm (person against nature); a boy cannot decide whether to tell the truth (inside the character)."),
        PQ("Using an invented outline: 'Ful is chosen to read at the school assembly. She is terrified. She practises in secret. On the day she freezes, then reads the first line and continues. Her classmates cheer.' Identify the climax and the resolution.",
           "The climax is the moment she freezes and then begins to read, the turning point. The resolution is the cheering classmates, which settles her fear."),
        SUM("A plot is a chain of events linked by cause and effect.",
            "The stages are exposition, rising action, climax, falling action and resolution.",
            "Conflict drives the plot; suspense keeps us reading; flashbacks give the past; a subplot runs beside the main story.",
            "The climax is the turning point, not simply the last event.",
            "In exams, give events from the text in the correct order and match them to the question."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3. Characterization
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Characterization",
    title="Characterization: how writers create characters",
    slug="lit1-characterization",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Writers show who a character is by telling, showing actions and speech, and using what others say. This lesson also separates round from flat characters, protagonist from antagonist, and shows how to write a character paragraph.",
    objectives=[
        "Define characterization and name direct and indirect methods",
        "Tell a protagonist from an antagonist and a round character from a flat one",
        "Find character traits from actions, speech and appearance in a short extract",
        "Write a character paragraph that uses evidence from the text",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Characterization", "Direct characterization", "Indirect characterization", "Protagonist", "Antagonist", "Round and flat characters", "Foil", "Trait"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("When you first meet a new teacher you notice what the teacher says, how the teacher walks into class and how the others speak about the teacher. Soon you have a picture of a kind, strict or funny person. "
          "Writers build a character in the same way, using words on the page."),
        D("**Characterization** is the way a writer creates and reveals a character. A **trait** is a quality of a character, such as brave, greedy or patient.", title="Characterization"),
        H("Direct and indirect characterization"),
        KEY("Two ways to show a character", "Good readers notice both.", cards=[
            ["Direct (telling)", "The writer or narrator simply tells us: 'Atem was a patient man.'"],
            ["Indirect (showing)", "We work it out from what the character says, does, thinks, looks like, and what others say about him or her."],
        ]),
        T("Indirect characterization uses five main clues. Remember them as **speech, actions, thoughts, appearance and what others say**."),
        T("Read this invented extract. It is not from any set text."),
        CODE("Mama Ngo did not look up when the inspector came in. She went on weighing the tomatoes, one pile after another, and called out the price in a voice that never changed.\n"
             "'You may sit,' she said at last, 'if you are going to buy.' Then she slipped a free tomato into the little girl's hand.",
             "text", title="Invented extract: Mama Ngo"),
        EX("Worked example 1: find the traits", intro="What traits of Mama Ngo does the extract show, and how?", steps=[
            S("Action: she does not look up", text="She goes on weighing. This shows she is confident and not afraid of authority."),
            S("Speech: 'You may sit, if you are going to buy'", text="She is firm and businesslike. She treats the inspector like any customer."),
            S("Action: the free tomato", text="She quietly gives to a child. This shows kindness hidden behind a strict manner."),
            S("Answer", text="Mama Ngo is confident, firm and businesslike, but also kind. The writer shows this through her actions and speech without ever using those words."),
        ]),
        H("Types of character"),
        D("The **protagonist** is the main character, the one whose story we follow. The **antagonist** is the character or force that opposes the protagonist. "
          "A **foil** is a character who is different from another, so that the differences show up.", title="Protagonist, antagonist, foil"),
        D("A **round** character is complex and has many sides, strengths and weaknesses, and may change. A **flat** character has one or two simple traits and does not change.", title="Round and flat"),
        T("Some characters are also **static**, meaning they stay the same, or **dynamic**, meaning they change in the course of the story. Not every antagonist is a villain: it can be a rival, a rule, or even fear."),
        H("Writing about a character"),
        KEY("A simple pattern for a character paragraph", "Make a point, prove it, explain it.", cards=[
            ["Point", "State a trait: 'Mama Ngo is firm.'"],
            ["Evidence", "Give a short example from the text (action or speech) or a few words, not a long copy."],
            ["Explanation", "Say what the evidence shows and why it matters."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a model paragraph", intro="Write a short paragraph on Mama Ngo's character using the invented extract.", steps=[
            S("Point", text="Mama Ngo is a confident and firm woman."),
            S("Evidence", text="She does not look up when the inspector enters and tells him to sit only 'if you are going to buy'."),
            S("Explanation", text="This shows she is not afraid of authority and runs her stall on her own terms."),
            S("Second point", text="Yet she is also kind: she slips a free tomato to a little girl. The contrast makes her a rounder, more believable character."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Naming a trait without proof", "Writing 'Mama Ngo is kind' is not enough. Always say what she does or says that shows it."),
        MISTAKE("Retelling the story instead of describing the character", "A character question asks 'what sort of person is X?' Use events only as evidence of a trait."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up characters or events", "The Board's report notes that candidates mixed up what belonged to different characters and different questions. Make a short character list from your own copy of each set text, with two or three traits and the moment that shows each."),
        MISTAKE("Bringing in personal experience", "Write about the character as the text presents him or her. Stories from your own family or town do not earn marks in a literature essay."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 1 asks what a short passage reveals about a character, or which word best describes a character's attitude. In Paper 2, questions on your set texts often ask you to describe a character and the part the character plays; the Board's report says character questions on a main figure were popular and well answered.", title="Exam link"),
        QUIZ("In the invented extract, what does the free tomato for the little girl show?",
             ["Mama Ngo is dishonest", "Mama Ngo is quietly kind", "Mama Ngo is afraid of the inspector", "Mama Ngo is careless with money"], 1,
             "Giving freely, and in secret, to a child shows warmth hidden behind her firm manner.",
             hint="Think about who the gift is for and who sees it.",
             feedback=["Nothing suggests dishonesty; the gift is open to the reader.", "", "She ignores the inspector, so she is not afraid.", "The extract does not suggest carelessness; she weighs carefully."]),
        QUIZ("A character who is simple, has one main trait and does not change is called:",
             ["Round", "Dynamic", "Flat", "Protagonist"], 2,
             "A flat character is simple and unchanging.",
             hint="The opposite of a complex character.",
             feedback=["A round character is complex.", "A dynamic character changes.", "", "The protagonist is the main character and can be round or flat."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Explain the difference between direct and indirect characterization.",
           "Direct: the writer tells us the trait outright. Indirect: we infer it from speech, actions, thoughts, appearance and what others say."),
        PQ("Read: 'Fon Tabe walked slowly into the hall. Everyone fell silent. He did not raise his voice, but when he asked a question, three elders answered at once.' What trait does this show, and how?",
           "Authority and respect. The silence, the elders answering quickly and his calm voice (actions and the reactions of others) all show that he commands respect without shouting."),
        PQ("Give the meaning of protagonist, antagonist and foil, with one invented example of each.",
           "Protagonist: main character, such as Ayuk in a story about a lost goat. Antagonist: the one who opposes, such as a rival neighbour. Foil: a contrasting character who highlights differences, such as a lazy brother beside a hardworking one."),
        SUM("Characterization is how a writer creates a character.",
            "Direct characterization tells; indirect characterization shows through speech, actions, thoughts, appearance and what others say.",
            "Protagonist, antagonist, foil, round and flat are the key terms.",
            "A good paragraph uses point, evidence and explanation.",
            "Stay with the text: no personal experience, and do not mix up characters or events."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4. Setting and point of view
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Setting and point of view",
    title="Setting and point of view",
    slug="lit1-setting-point-of-view",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Setting is the time and place of a story and the atmosphere they create; point of view is who tells it. This lesson explains first-person, third-person limited and omniscient narration and what each does to the reader.",
    objectives=[
        "Define setting and explain how it affects mood, character and conflict",
        "Distinguish first-person, third-person limited and omniscient narration",
        "Identify the point of view of a short passage from its pronouns and what it reveals",
        "Explain the effect of a writer's choice of narrator",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Setting", "Atmosphere", "Point of view", "Narrator", "First-person", "Third-person limited", "Omniscient", "Reliable and unreliable narrator"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A story about a boy waiting for exam results feels very different if it takes place in a quiet village at dusk or on a loud, crowded street at noon. "
          "It also feels different if the boy tells us himself ('I felt sick') or an outsider describes him ('he looked pale'). These two choices, where and when, and who tells it, are setting and point of view."),
        D("**Setting** is the time and place in which a story happens, together with the surroundings, customs and atmosphere. Time can mean the season, the year or the period in history.", title="Setting"),
        T("Setting is not just a backdrop. It can create **atmosphere** (the feeling around the scene), shape the characters' lives and even cause the conflict. "
          "A drought in a farming village, for example, can start a quarrel over water."),
        KEY("What setting can do", "When you meet a setting, ask four questions.", cards=[
            ["Where and when?", "Place, time of day, season, period."],
            ["What atmosphere?", "Peaceful, tense, gloomy, lively?"],
            ["What does it tell us?", "About the characters' lives, customs and values."],
            ["Does it cause events?", "Does the place or time help to start or shape the conflict?"],
        ]),
        T("Here is an invented extract. It is not from any set text."),
        CODE("The bus groaned up the hill into the fog. Tabe pressed his forehead to the cold window and counted the roadside stalls: "
             "a woman roasting corn, a boy selling oranges from a bowl, a man asleep beside a pile of firewood. Behind him someone was singing softly. "
             "He did not turn round. He was thinking of the envelope in his pocket, and of the words he had practised and would never say.",
             "text", title="Invented extract: the bus"),
        EX("Worked example 1: setting and atmosphere", intro="Describe the setting of the invented extract and the atmosphere it creates.", steps=[
            S("Find place and time", text="A bus climbing a hill early in the day (fog, cold window), passing roadside stalls in a highland region."),
            S("Pick out details", text="Fog, cold window, a man asleep, soft singing, stalls of corn and oranges."),
            S("Name the atmosphere", text="Quiet, a little sad and uncertain. The fog and the cold suit Tabe's worry about the envelope."),
            S("Answer", text="The setting is a bus on a foggy hill road. The cold, misty, quiet details create an atmosphere of uncertainty that matches Tabe's inner worry."),
        ]),
        H("Point of view"),
        D("**Point of view** is the position from which a story is told: who the narrator is and how much the narrator knows. The **narrator** is the voice that tells the story.", title="Point of view"),
        KEY("Three main points of view", "Check the pronouns first.", cards=[
            ["First-person", "The narrator is a character who says 'I' or 'we'. We see only what that person sees and feels."],
            ["Third-person limited", "The narrator is outside the story and says 'he' or 'she', but stays close to one character's thoughts."],
            ["Third-person omniscient", "The narrator knows everything: the thoughts of all characters, past and future. 'Omniscient' means all-knowing."],
        ]),
        T("The invented bus extract is third-person limited. The narrator says 'Tabe' and 'he', and tells us what he is thinking, but not what the singer behind him thinks."),
        T("The same moment in first person would read: 'I pressed my forehead to the cold window. I was thinking of the envelope in my pocket.' "
          "First person feels close and personal, but we may not be told the whole truth. A **reliable narrator** is trustworthy; an **unreliable narrator** may be mistaken, biased or lying."),
        EX("Worked example 2: identify the point of view", intro="Name the point of view of each invented sentence. (a) 'I never told my mother where I was going.' (b) 'Ful opened the door and wondered whether anyone had noticed her.' "
           "(c) 'The chief smiled, though in his heart he feared the council; and the council, for its part, feared the chief.'", steps=[
            S("(a)", text="'I' tells us the narrator is a character in the story: first-person."),
            S("(b)", text="'Ful' and 'her' are third-person; we know only Ful's thoughts: third-person limited."),
            S("(c)", text="The narrator knows the secret fears of both the chief and the council: omniscient."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Saying 'the narrator' is the author", "The narrator is the voice the author creates. In a first-person novel the 'I' is a character, not the writer."),
        MISTAKE("Listing places without effect", "A setting answer must say what the place does: the mood it makes, what it shows about the characters, how it shapes events."),
        MISTAKE("Guessing the point of view from the topic", "Decide from the pronouns and from how much the narrator knows, not from what the story is about."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 1 (multiple choice) often gives a short passage and asks for the point of view or the effect of the setting. In Paper 2 a question on style may ask why the writer chose a particular narrator; the Board's report notes that candidates were uncomfortable with a question testing style and themes, so practise explaining the effect, not just naming the technique.", title="Exam link"),
        QUIZ("'We watched the road all night and nobody spoke.' The point of view is:",
             ["Third-person limited", "Omniscient", "First-person", "Second-person singular"], 2,
             "'We' shows the narrator is one of the characters, speaking from inside the story.",
             hint="Look at the pronoun 'we'.",
             feedback=["Third-person uses he, she or they.", "An omniscient narrator stands outside and knows everything.", "", "Second-person uses 'you' to the reader; 'we' is first-person plural."]),
        QUIZ("Which is the best description of a 'setting'?",
             ["The sequence of events", "The time, place and atmosphere of the story", "The way a character is shown", "The writer's attitude"], 1,
             "Setting is when and where the story happens, with the atmosphere around it.",
             hint="It answers 'where and when?'",
             feedback=["That is the plot.", "", "That is characterization.", "That is tone."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Define point of view and name three types.", "Point of view is the position from which a story is told. Types: first-person, third-person limited, third-person omniscient."),
        PQ("In the invented bus extract, give two details of setting and say what each suggests.",
           "The fog suggests uncertainty or mystery. The sleeping man and soft singing suggest a quiet, calm journey in contrast to Tabe's inner worry."),
        PQ("Rewrite the sentence 'Tabe thought of the envelope in his pocket' in the first person and say what changes for the reader.",
           "'I thought of the envelope in my pocket.' The reader is closer to the narrator's feelings but sees only what the narrator knows."),
        SUM("Setting is the time, place and atmosphere of a story. It can shape mood, character and conflict.",
            "Point of view is who tells the story and how much that voice knows.",
            "First-person uses I or we; third-person limited stays close to one character; omniscient knows everything.",
            "The narrator is not the author.",
            "In exams, always say what the setting or the narrator does, not just what it is."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5. Diction, tone and mood
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Diction, tone and mood",
    title="Diction, tone and mood",
    slug="lit1-diction-tone-mood",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Diction is the writer's choice of words; tone is the writer's attitude; mood is the feeling created in the reader. This lesson shows how word choice, denotation and connotation shape tone and mood.",
    objectives=[
        "Define diction, tone and mood and tell them apart",
        "Explain denotation and connotation",
        "Recognise formal, informal and colloquial diction",
        "Name the tone of a short passage and support it with words from the passage",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Diction", "Denotation", "Connotation", "Register", "Tone", "Mood", "Atmosphere"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Compare three ways of saying the same thing. 'He is thin.' 'He is slim.' 'He is skinny.' All three describe a body, but 'slim' sounds pleasant, 'skinny' sounds rude, and 'thin' sounds neutral. "
          "The writer who picks one of these words is making a choice. That choice is called diction."),
        D("**Diction** is a writer's choice of words and the style that results. Careful diction makes meaning, feeling and music.", title="Diction"),
        KEY("Two meanings of a word", "Every word has both.", cards=[
            ["Denotation", "The plain dictionary meaning. 'Home' denotes a place where one lives."],
            ["Connotation", "The feelings and ideas that surround a word. 'Home' also suggests warmth, family and safety."],
        ]),
        T("Writers pick words for their connotation. If a writer calls a house 'a hut' instead of 'a home', the denotation is nearly the same but the feeling is different."),
        H("Kinds of diction"),
        KEY("Register: how formal is the language?", "Register is the level of formality chosen for the situation.", cards=[
            ["Formal", "Careful, polite, complete sentences. 'I would be grateful if you could assist me.'"],
            ["Informal / colloquial", "Everyday, relaxed, may use slang or short forms. 'Can you help me small?'"],
            ["Concrete and abstract", "Concrete words name things we can sense (rain, drum). Abstract words name ideas (freedom, fear)."],
        ]),
        T("Writers sometimes use Pidgin English, local words or dialect inside English text to show a character's background. This is a deliberate choice of diction, "
          "and it tells us who is speaking and in what situation."),
        H("Tone and mood"),
        D("**Tone** is the writer's or speaker's attitude towards the subject or the reader, such as serious, humorous, bitter, loving, mocking or hopeful. "
          "**Mood** is the feeling or atmosphere created in the reader, such as calm, tense, sad or joyful.", title="Tone and mood"),
        T("An easy way to remember: tone is how the writer feels; mood is how the reader feels. A bitter, mocking tone can leave the reader feeling uneasy (mood)."),
        CODE("The pot was empty, and Mother said nothing.\nShe wiped the table that was already clean,\nand the whole house learned to breathe quietly.",
             "text", title="Invented poem fragment: the empty pot"),
        EX("Worked example 1: find the tone and mood", intro="Name the tone and the mood of the invented fragment and prove it from the diction.", steps=[
            S("Read the key words", text="'empty', 'said nothing', 'wiped the table that was already clean', 'learned to breathe quietly'."),
            S("Tone", text="The speaker is gentle and restrained. There is no complaint, only sad, quiet observation. Tone: sad and sympathetic."),
            S("Mood", text="The silence and the quiet breathing leave the reader with a heavy, tense, sorrowful mood."),
            S("Answer", text="Tone: restrained and sympathetic. Mood: heavy and sorrowful. The words 'empty' and 'quietly' and the action of wiping a clean table show the family's silent hunger."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: choose the word", intro="A writer describes a thief. Which word has the most negative connotation: 'visitor', 'stranger', 'intruder'? Explain.", steps=[
            S("Compare denotation", text="All three name a person who comes from outside."),
            S("Compare connotation", text="'Visitor' is welcome; 'stranger' is neutral or unknown; 'intruder' suggests someone who enters without right."),
            S("Answer", text="'Intruder' is the most negative. The writer chose it to make us feel suspicion."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up tone and mood", "Tone belongs to the writer or speaker; mood belongs to the atmosphere and the reader. A question that asks for 'the poet's tone' needs the poet's attitude, not the reader's feeling."),
        MISTAKE("Giving a vague tone word", "'Sad' or 'good' are too weak. Use precise words such as sorrowful, bitter, ironic, tender, playful, defiant, nostalgic, then prove it with words from the text."),
        MISTAKE("Naming without quoting", "Always support tone with a few words from the passage. In a set-text answer stay with the text, not with your own opinion or experience."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 1 (multiple choice) often asks for the tone or mood of a short extract, or what a particular word suggests. The Board's report says that candidates were uncomfortable with questions on style, so practise saying what a word choice does.", title="Exam link"),
        QUIZ("The 'connotation' of a word is:",
             ["Its dictionary meaning", "The feelings and ideas associated with it", "Its spelling", "How many syllables it has"], 1,
             "Connotation is the extra feeling a word carries beyond its plain meaning.",
             hint="Compare 'home' and 'house'.",
             feedback=["That is denotation.", "", "Spelling is not a meaning.", "Syllables belong to sound, not connotation."]),
        QUIZ("'Mood' refers to:",
             ["The writer's attitude to the subject", "The atmosphere or feeling created for the reader", "The choice of difficult words", "The number of stanzas"], 1,
             "Mood is the feeling we, as readers, get from a passage.",
             hint="Tone is the writer; mood is the...",
             feedback=["That is tone.", "", "That is part of diction, not mood.", "That is structure."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Explain the difference between denotation and connotation, using the word 'snake' as an example.",
           "Denotation: a long, legless reptile. Connotation: danger, fear, or deceit, which is why a writer may call a treacherous person a snake."),
        PQ("Give two words that could replace 'house' to make the tone warm and two to make it cold.",
           "Warm: home, haven. Cold: shack, dwelling (or block). Answers may vary if the connotations are explained."),
        PQ("Read: 'He sat, as he did every evening, counting the coins he did not have.' State the tone and give one word or phrase that proves it.",
           "Tone: wry, sympathetic or bitter-sad. The phrase 'coins he did not have' shows gentle irony about his poverty."),
        SUM("Diction is word choice. It carries denotation (plain meaning) and connotation (feeling).",
            "Register can be formal or informal; words can be concrete or abstract.",
            "Tone is the writer's attitude; mood is the atmosphere felt by the reader.",
            "Name tone and mood precisely and always prove them from the words.",
            "Style questions need effect: what does this word choice do?"),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6. Irony
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Irony and figurative devices",
    title="Irony: verbal, situational and dramatic",
    slug="lit1-irony",
    minutes=20,
    summary="Irony is a gap between what is said and what is meant, or between what we expect and what happens. This lesson explains verbal, situational and dramatic irony, separates irony from sarcasm and shows how to explain its effect.",
    objectives=[
        "Define irony and name its three main types",
        "Identify verbal, situational and dramatic irony in short invented examples",
        "Distinguish irony from sarcasm and coincidence",
        "Explain the effect irony has on a reader",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Irony", "Verbal irony", "Situational irony", "Dramatic irony", "Sarcasm", "Satire", "Understatement"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A man stands in the pouring rain, completely soaked, and says, 'Lovely weather today!' Everyone knows he means the opposite. "
          "A fire station burns down. A teacher who tells students to be on time arrives late for the final exam. Each of these has a gap between expectation and reality. That gap is irony."),
        D("**Irony** is a contrast between what is said and what is meant, or between what is expected and what actually happens, or between what a character knows and what the audience knows.", title="Irony"),
        KEY("Three main types", "Learn the key question for each.", cards=[
            ["Verbal irony", "Someone says the opposite of what they mean. 'Lovely weather!' in a storm."],
            ["Situational irony", "What happens is the opposite of what was expected. A fire station burns down."],
            ["Dramatic irony", "The audience or reader knows something that a character does not."],
        ]),
        T("**Sarcasm** is a sharp, mocking kind of verbal irony meant to hurt. 'Well done, genius' said to someone who has just made a mistake is sarcasm. "
          "Not all irony is cruel: it can be gentle or funny. **Understatement** (saying less than is true, as in 'It was a bit warm' about a heatwave) is another way of using verbal irony."),
        D("**Satire** is writing that uses irony, humour and exaggeration to criticise foolishness or wrongdoing in people or society.", title="Satire"),
        H("How to explain irony"),
        T("To explain irony in an answer, follow three steps: say what the surface meaning is, say what the real meaning or fact is, and explain the effect."),
        EX("Worked example 1: name the type", intro="Name the type of irony in each invented example. (a) A student who has failed every test says with a grin, 'Yes, I am clearly the cleverest in the class.' "
           "(b) A sign on the door of a school library says 'Silence! Library' and below it a bell rings loudly. (c) A poor boy sells his only bicycle to buy his sister a gift. She sells her gift to buy him a bicycle helmet. "
           "(d) In a play the audience has seen that the king's cup is poisoned, but the king raises it to drink.", steps=[
            S("(a)", text="The student says the opposite of what is true and means the opposite. Verbal irony (a joke against himself)."),
            S("(b)", text="A notice calling for silence is accompanied by noise: a contrast between expectation and what happens. Situational irony."),
            S("(c)", text="Each gives up what the other needs to make the gift useless: situational irony, the opposite of what each hoped."),
            S("(d)", text="The audience knows about the poison and the king does not: dramatic irony."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: effect of irony", intro="Explain why a writer might use dramatic irony in (d).", steps=[
            S("State the irony", text="We know the cup is poisoned, but the king does not."),
            S("Effect on the audience", text="We feel tension and fear for the king. We want to shout a warning, which keeps us gripped."),
            S("Link to meaning", text="It can also make us think about trust and betrayal in the story."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Calling every sad or unlucky event ironic", "Bad luck is not irony. Irony needs a gap: something opposite to what was expected, said or known. A person simply falling ill is misfortune, not irony."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing irony with coincidence", "A coincidence is two events that happen together by chance. Irony involves a contrast of meaning or outcome."),
        MISTAKE("Stating irony without effect", "'This is irony' earns little. Say what the reader feels or learns: humour, tension, criticism, sympathy."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 1 (multiple choice) often gives a short passage and asks which kind of irony it shows, or what it achieves. In Paper 2 you may meet irony in the language of your set plays and novels; practise naming it and explaining its effect, using only the text.", title="Exam link"),
        QUIZ("A teacher shouts 'Be quiet!' so loudly that the whole school hears. This is best called:",
             ["Dramatic irony", "Situational irony", "Alliteration", "Personification"], 1,
             "The behaviour is the opposite of the request: noise to get quiet. That is a contrast between expectation and what happens.",
             hint="Is there a gap between what is wanted and what happens?",
             feedback=["Dramatic irony needs the audience to know something that a character does not.", "", "Alliteration repeats consonant sounds.", "Personification gives human qualities to non-human things."]),
        QUIZ("What is 'dramatic irony'?",
             ["Saying the opposite of what you mean", "The audience knows something a character does not", "An event that is very sad", "A sudden change of scene"], 1,
             "Dramatic irony depends on the audience's greater knowledge.",
             hint="Think of a horror film where the viewer knows the monster is behind the door.",
             feedback=["That is verbal irony.", "", "Sadness is not irony.", "A change of scene is a matter of staging."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Define irony and name its three main types.", "Irony is a gap between expectation and reality. Types: verbal, situational and dramatic."),
        PQ("Give an example of verbal irony and an example of situational irony. Invent your own.",
           "Verbal: a farmer in a drought says, 'What a wonderful, wet season!' Situational: a lifeguard drowns in a shallow pool."),
        PQ("Explain how irony differs from sarcasm.", "Sarcasm is a sharp, mocking form of verbal irony meant to hurt. Irony in general is wider: it includes gentle, funny and situational forms, and dramatic irony."),
        SUM("Irony is a contrast between expectation and reality.",
            "Verbal irony: saying the opposite. Situational irony: an unexpected opposite outcome. Dramatic irony: the audience knows more than a character.",
            "Sarcasm is a cutting form of verbal irony; satire uses irony to criticise.",
            "Irony is not just bad luck or coincidence.",
            "Always explain the effect of irony in an answer."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 7. Figurative devices
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Irony and figurative devices",
    title="Figurative language: simile, metaphor, personification and sound devices",
    slug="lit1-figurative-devices",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Figurative language says one thing in terms of another to make writing vivid. This lesson covers simile, metaphor, personification, hyperbole, oxymoron, apostrophe, onomatopoeia, alliteration and assonance, and how to explain their effect.",
    objectives=[
        "Define figurative language and tell it from literal language",
        "Identify simile, metaphor, personification, hyperbole, oxymoron and apostrophe",
        "Identify the sound devices alliteration, assonance and onomatopoeia",
        "Explain the effect of a device in two or three sentences",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Figurative language", "Simile", "Metaphor", "Personification", "Hyperbole", "Oxymoron", "Apostrophe", "Alliteration", "Assonance", "Onomatopoeia"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("If your friend says 'My bag weighs a ton', you do not think it weighs a thousand kilograms. You understand that he means it is very heavy. "
          "When we say one thing in a special way to give a stronger picture or feeling, we are using **figurative language**."),
        D("**Literal** language means exactly what it says. **Figurative** language uses words in a non-literal way to create a picture, a feeling or an emphasis. The special ways are called **figures of speech** or devices.", title="Figurative language"),
        H("Comparing things"),
        KEY("Simile and metaphor", "Both compare two different things.", cards=[
            ["Simile", "A comparison using 'like' or 'as'. 'Her voice was like warm water.'"],
            ["Metaphor", "A direct comparison without 'like' or 'as'. 'Her voice was warm water.'"],
        ]),
        KEY("Personification and apostrophe", "Both give life to what is not alive.", cards=[
            ["Personification", "Giving human qualities to a thing, idea or animal. 'The wind whispered at the door.'"],
            ["Apostrophe", "Speaking directly to someone absent, dead or to a thing. 'O River, tell me where you go!'"],
        ]),
        H("Exaggeration and contradiction"),
        KEY("Hyperbole, understatement and oxymoron", "These play with size and sense.", cards=[
            ["Hyperbole", "Deliberate exaggeration. 'I have told you a thousand times.'"],
            ["Understatement", "Saying less than is true. 'It was a little warm', about a heatwave."],
            ["Oxymoron", "Two opposite words together. 'Bitter sweet', 'a loud silence'."],
        ]),
        H("Sound devices"),
        KEY("Alliteration, assonance, onomatopoeia", "These work on the ear.", cards=[
            ["Alliteration", "Repeated consonant sound at the start of nearby words. 'Big black bags'."],
            ["Assonance", "Repeated vowel sound in nearby words. 'The rain came down in the lane.'"],
            ["Onomatopoeia", "A word that imitates a sound. 'Buzz', 'crash', 'drip'."],
        ]),
        D("**Imagery** is the pictures in words that appeal to the senses of sight, sound, smell, taste and touch. **Symbolism** is the use of an object, person or action to stand for an idea. "
          "Both are explored further in the poetry section of this course.", title="Imagery and symbolism"),
        T("Spotting a device is only half the job. Examiners also expect you to say what it does. A short pattern is **Name, Quote, Effect**: name the device, give the words, and say what picture or feeling it creates."),
        CODE("The night was a black cloth pulled over the hill.\nThe river muttered to itself and the drums went boom, boom, boom.\nI have waited a million years for the morning.",
             "text", title="Invented practice lines"),
        EX("Worked example 1: find the devices", intro="Name the device in each line of the invented practice lines.", steps=[
            S("Line 1", text="'The night was a black cloth' compares night to cloth without 'like' or 'as': a metaphor."),
            S("Line 2", text="'The river muttered' gives a river a human action: personification. 'Boom, boom, boom' imitates a drum sound: onomatopoeia."),
            S("Line 3", text="'A million years' is an obvious exaggeration: hyperbole."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: explain the effect", intro="Explain the effect of the metaphor 'The night was a black cloth pulled over the hill.'", steps=[
            S("Name and quote", text="The metaphor compares night to a black cloth."),
            S("Say what it shows", text="A cloth covers things completely, so the dark seems to hide the whole hill."),
            S("Link to feeling", text="It makes the night heavy and mysterious, and sets a quiet, uneasy mood."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Only naming the device", "A mark is usually earned by the explanation. After the name give the effect: what picture, feeling or idea it creates."),
        MISTAKE("Calling every comparison a metaphor", "If the sentence has 'like' or 'as', it is a simile. If it states 'A is B', it is a metaphor."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing alliteration with assonance", "Alliteration repeats consonant sounds (usually at the start of words); assonance repeats vowel sounds."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 1 (multiple choice) very often asks you to name a device from a short line and sometimes to say what it achieves. In Paper 2 the Board's report notes that candidates were uncomfortable with a question testing style and themes, so link every device to meaning or mood.", title="Exam link"),
        QUIZ("'The classroom was a beehive.' This is a:",
             ["Simile", "Metaphor", "Personification", "Onomatopoeia"], 1,
             "It says one thing is another without 'like' or 'as': metaphor.",
             hint="Is there 'like' or 'as' in the sentence?",
             feedback=["A simile would say 'like a beehive'.", "", "No human quality is given to the classroom.", "No sound is imitated."]),
        QUIZ("'She sells sea shells' has repeated 's' sounds at the start of words. This is:",
             ["Assonance", "Hyperbole", "Alliteration", "Oxymoron"], 2,
             "Repeated consonant sounds at the beginning of nearby words are alliteration.",
             hint="Is the repeated sound a vowel or a consonant?",
             feedback=["Assonance repeats vowel sounds.", "Hyperbole is exaggeration.", "", "An oxymoron joins two opposite words."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Identify the device: (a) 'as cold as a stone'; (b) 'The sun smiled on us'; (c) 'a deafening silence'.",
           "(a) simile; (b) personification; (c) oxymoron."),
        PQ("Write one line of your own using hyperbole and one using personification.",
           "Examples: 'I could eat a whole goat' (hyperbole). 'The old truck coughed and refused to climb' (personification)."),
        PQ("Explain the effect of the simile 'Her laughter was like rain on a tin roof'.",
           "It compares laughter to a lively, rattling sound. We hear a bright, cheerful, noisy laughter and feel warmth."),
        SUM("Figurative language says things in a non-literal way to make writing vivid.",
            "Simile uses like or as; metaphor states that one thing is another; personification gives human qualities.",
            "Hyperbole exaggerates; understatement lessens; oxymoron joins opposites; apostrophe speaks to someone absent.",
            "Alliteration repeats consonants, assonance repeats vowels, onomatopoeia imitates sound.",
            "Always follow the name of a device with its effect."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 8. Reading a poem
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Reading a poem",
    title="Reading a poem: a five-question method",
    slug="lit1-reading-a-poem",
    minutes=24,
    summary="A step-by-step way to read any poem: read twice, paraphrase, then ask who speaks, to whom, about what, how and why. The lesson also explains how to write about a poem and stay with the text, using an invented poem.",
    objectives=[
        "Read a poem in stages: first for sense, then for craft",
        "Paraphrase a poem in plain prose",
        "Use five questions (who, to whom, what, how, why) to analyse a poem",
        "Write a short point-evidence-explanation paragraph and stay with the text",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Paraphrase", "Speaker", "Subject", "Stanza", "Title", "Devices", "Theme", "Staying with the text"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Many students think poems are 'hard' because they read them once, quickly, and then guess. A poem is short, but it is packed. "
          "A good reader reads it slowly, several times, and asks the right questions each time."),
        T("In the Board's 2023 report for Literature in English, Paper 2 has a poetry section with two questions. The report names the anthology as a modern anthology by Hans Bokwe Itoe in the new syllabus, "
          "and a Cameroon anthology by Bole Butake in the old syllabus. It names one poem in the new anthology: 'I am an African Child'. "
          "Your teacher will tell you which anthology and which poems your class studies. **Always use your own copy of the anthology.** In this course we practise the method on invented poems, so you can apply it to every poem in your own book."),
        D("A **paraphrase** is a poem's meaning put into plain prose, in your own words, line by line or idea by idea. A paraphrase is not a summary of your feelings: it says what the poem says.", title="Paraphrase"),
        H("The method"),
        IMG("f5lit-1-read-steps.svg", alt="Six boxes in order: read twice, paraphrase, who speaks, to whom and about what, how (devices), why (theme)",
            caption="Figure 1. Reading a poem step by step."),
        KEY("Five questions for any poem", "Ask them after two readings and a paraphrase.", cards=[
            ["Who speaks?", "The speaker or persona: an observer, a child, an old person, the poet?"],
            ["To whom?", "The listener: the reader, a person, a place, no one?"],
            ["About what?", "The subject and situation, in plain words."],
            ["How?", "Imagery, figures of speech, sound, rhythm, form and tone."],
            ["Why?", "The theme or message; what the poem makes us feel or think."],
        ]),
        T("Here is an invented poem. It is not from any anthology."),
        CODE("The road is a red scar across the hill.\nDust stands up behind each passing truck\nand settles on the leaves like a grey sleep.\nA girl walks on with water on her head.\nHer steps are slow. She does not look back.",
             "text", title="Invented poem: Dry Season Road"),
        EX("Worked example 1: apply the five questions", intro="Analyse 'Dry Season Road' with the five questions.", steps=[
            S("Read twice and paraphrase", text="On a dusty road in the dry season, trucks raise dust that covers the leaves. A girl carries water on her head and walks slowly without looking back."),
            S("Who speaks and to whom?", text="An observer who watches from a distance; the poem seems to address the reader quietly."),
            S("About what?", text="A girl's journey with water in the dry season."),
            S("How?", text="Metaphor 'a red scar' (the road as a wound on the land), personification 'dust stands up', simile 'like a grey sleep', short, plain sentences at the end."),
            S("Why?", text="The poem shows hardship and quiet endurance. The final short sentences give her steady dignity."),
        ]),
        H("Writing about a poem"),
        T("Examiners want a **detailed account** of the poem or the part named in the question. That means you should know what happens in the poem, stanza by stanza, and refer to the words. "
          "Use the pattern **point, evidence, explanation**: make a point, give a few words from the poem as proof, and explain the effect."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a model paragraph", intro="How does the poet suggest hardship in 'Dry Season Road'?", steps=[
            S("Point", text="The poet suggests hardship through images of damage and dryness."),
            S("Evidence", text="The road is 'a red scar' and the dust settles 'like a grey sleep'."),
            S("Explanation", text="A scar suggests a wound that has healed badly, so the land looks hurt. The grey dust covers everything and makes the scene lifeless."),
            S("Link to the girl", text="Against this setting the girl's slow steps and her load of water show quiet endurance."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Bringing in personal experience", "The Board's report says that some candidates, asked about a named poem, wrote about their own experiences of the subject instead of giving a detailed account of the poem. Stay with the text: say what the poem says and how it says it."),
        MISTAKE("Writing about only the favourite poems", "The report says one question was more popular and better answered because the poem and theme were familiar, while the other poem was avoided. Read every poem in your anthology so the question does not catch you out."),
        MISTAKE("Skipping the paraphrase", "If you cannot say what the poem says in plain words, you cannot say anything reliable about how it says it. Paraphrase first."),
        MISTAKE("Talking only about the poet's life", "The report advises teachers to focus on analysis of the text rather than on authors' backgrounds. Use the poem's words as your evidence."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 1 (multiple choice) includes poetry questions and may ask for the meaning, the speaker, a device or the tone of a passage. Paper 2 Section C asks you to write about poems from the anthology. Practise this method on every poem in your own book.", title="Exam link"),
        QUIZ("What is a paraphrase of a poem?",
             ["A list of devices in the poem", "The poem's meaning in plain prose, in your own words", "Your opinion of the poet", "A copy of the poem in your own handwriting"], 1,
             "A paraphrase says what the poem says in plain prose.",
             hint="Think of it as translating the poem into ordinary language.",
             feedback=["That is analysis of devices, not paraphrase.", "", "An opinion is not a paraphrase.", "Copying does not explain meaning."]),
        QUIZ("A question asks about a named poem in the anthology. Which answer stays closest to what the Board expects?",
             ["A story about your own journey to the village", "A detailed account of the poem with short quotations", "A biography of the poet", "A list of other poems on the same subject"], 1,
             "The report stresses a detailed account of the poem, not personal experience.",
             hint="What did the report say candidates did wrongly?",
             feedback=["Personal experience is not what the question asks.", "", "The report advises focusing on the text, not on the author's background.", "The question is about the named poem, not others."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Write a plain-prose paraphrase of 'Dry Season Road'.",
           "On a dry-season road, trucks raise dust that coats the leaves. A girl carries water on her head and walks slowly without looking back."),
        PQ("Identify one metaphor and one simile in 'Dry Season Road' and say what each suggests.",
           "Metaphor: 'a red scar' suggests the road as a wound on the land. Simile: 'like a grey sleep' suggests the dust makes the leaves look lifeless and still."),
        PQ("List the five questions you would ask of any poem.",
           "Who speaks? To whom? About what? How (devices)? Why (theme and effect)?"),
        SUM("Read a poem at least twice, then paraphrase it in plain prose.",
            "Ask: who speaks, to whom, about what, how, and why.",
            "Write point, evidence, explanation, with short quotations.",
            "Stay with the text: no personal experience essays and no life stories of the poet.",
            "Know every poem in your own anthology, not just the favourites."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 9. Imagery and symbolism
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Imagery and symbolism",
    title="Imagery and symbolism in poetry",
    slug="lit1-imagery-symbolism",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Imagery is language that appeals to the senses; a symbol is a thing that stands for an idea. This lesson shows how to find images and symbols in a poem, explain what they suggest and write about them with evidence.",
    objectives=[
        "Define imagery and name the types of sensory image",
        "Define symbol and tell it from a simple object or a metaphor",
        "Explain what an image or symbol adds to a poem",
        "Write a short point-evidence-explanation paragraph on imagery or symbolism",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Imagery", "Visual image", "Auditory image", "Tactile image", "Symbol", "Literal and symbolic meaning", "Motif", "Pattern of imagery"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("When someone says 'my grandmother's kitchen', you may smell smoke and spices, hear a pot bubbling and see a blackened wall. The words have made pictures and sensations in your mind. "
          "Poets work on this principle: they use few words to wake many senses."),
        D("**Imagery** is the use of words that create pictures or sensations: sight (visual), sound (auditory), touch (tactile), smell (olfactory) and taste (gustatory). "
          "An **image** is one such picture or sensation in words.", title="Imagery"),
        KEY("The senses in imagery", "Ask which sense each image uses.", cards=[
            ["Visual", "'a red scar across the hill'"],
            ["Auditory", "'the rain drums on the tin'"],
            ["Tactile", "'cold, wet cloth against the skin'"],
            ["Olfactory and gustatory", "'smell of smoke and ripe mangoes', 'the bitter taste of the leaf'"],
        ]),
        D("A **symbol** is an object, person, place or action that stands for something bigger, usually an idea. A rose may be only a flower in a garden, but in a poem it may stand for love or beauty. "
          "A **motif** is an image, symbol or idea that repeats in a poem or text.", title="Symbol"),
        T("Not every object is a symbol. An object becomes symbolic when the poem gives it weight: it is described with care, it repeats, or it ends the poem. Always ask: what does it stand for in this poem?"),
        T("Here is an invented poem. It is not from any anthology."),
        CODE("My grandmother's calabash hangs by the door,\ncracked at the lip, mended with a thread of wire.\nIt has carried water, palm wine, and the news\nof three burials and one wedding.\nNobody drinks from it now,\nyet nobody will take it down.",
             "text", title="Invented poem: The Calabash"),
        EX("Worked example 1: find the imagery", intro="Pick out the images in 'The Calabash' and say which sense they appeal to.", steps=[
            S("Visual images", text="'cracked at the lip' and 'mended with a thread of wire' let us see the damaged, repaired calabash hanging by the door."),
            S("Other senses", text="'water, palm wine' invite taste and touch. The poem is mainly visual and tactile, with a hint of taste."),
            S("Answer", text="Visual images of the cracked and mended calabash; images of drinking that suggest taste and touch."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: what is the symbol?", intro="What does the calabash symbolise in the poem?", steps=[
            S("Literal meaning", text="A real calabash, an old container that has been used for water and palm wine."),
            S("Clues that it is more than a container", text="It has carried 'the news of three burials and one wedding', so it holds the family's memories. It is cracked but mended, so it has survived. Nobody uses it but nobody removes it."),
            S("Symbolic meaning", text="The calabash stands for family memory and the continuity between generations, and for the way people keep the past close even when it is no longer used."),
            S("Answer", text="The calabash symbolises family memory and continuity, valued even though it is cracked and no longer used."),
        ]),
        T("Notice the difference between image and symbol. The 'cracked lip' is an image. The calabash as a whole, repeated and weighted, is the symbol."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a model paragraph", intro="How does the poet use the calabash to show that the past is still valued?", steps=[
            S("Point", text="The poet shows that the past is still valued through the calabash."),
            S("Evidence", text="It is 'cracked at the lip' but 'mended with a thread of wire', and 'nobody will take it down'."),
            S("Explanation", text="The repair shows care. The last line shows that though it has no use, the family cannot let go, because it holds memory."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Saying a symbol 'means' one fixed thing", "A symbol takes its meaning from the poem. Do not use a fixed 'dictionary of symbols'. Explain what it stands for here, using clues in the poem."),
        MISTAKE("Listing images without explaining them", "Naming 'visual imagery' earns little. Say what the image makes us see or feel and why the poet needs it."),
        MISTAKE("Inventing meanings", "Do not claim a meaning you cannot support with words from the poem. In your own anthology, stay with what the text gives you. If your teacher offers a reading, note the clue in the poem that supports it."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 1 (multiple choice) asks you to name an image, say what it suggests, or choose what an object stands for in a short poem. Paper 2 Section C questions on your anthology may ask about the images or symbols the poet uses; always quote briefly and explain.", title="Exam link"),
        QUIZ("The words 'the rain drums on the tin' appeal mainly to the sense of:",
             ["Sight", "Sound", "Taste", "Smell"], 1,
             "'Drums' and 'rain on tin' call up sound: an auditory image.",
             hint="Which sense does 'drum' belong to?",
             feedback=["There is no picture-based word here; the key word 'drums' is about hearing.", "", "Nothing is tasted.", "Nothing is smelled."]),
        QUIZ("In a poem, a symbol is:",
             ["Any object mentioned", "A thing that stands for an idea", "A rhyming word", "A line that repeats"], 1,
             "A symbol is a concrete thing that stands for a larger idea.",
             hint="It points beyond itself.",
             feedback=["Many objects are only literal.", "", "Rhyme is a sound pattern.", "A repeated line is a refrain, not necessarily a symbol."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Define imagery and name three types of sensory image.", "Imagery is language that creates sensory pictures. Types: visual, auditory, tactile (also olfactory, gustatory)."),
        PQ("In 'The Calabash', explain what the mended wire shows.", "It shows care and the wish to keep the calabash, even though it is damaged. It suggests that the family values it and keeps the past alive."),
        PQ("Choose an everyday object in your home area (a hoe, a lamp, a drum, a school bag). Say what it could symbolise in a poem and which words in the poem would make that clear.",
           "For example: a lamp could symbolise hope or learning if the poem describes it burning late while a child studies. The clues would be the words describing light against darkness."),
        SUM("Imagery is sensory language: visual, auditory, tactile, olfactory and gustatory.",
            "A symbol is a thing that stands for an idea; its meaning comes from the poem.",
            "A motif is a repeated image or idea.",
            "Always name, quote and explain the effect.",
            "Do not force meanings that the words of the poem do not support."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 10. Rhythm, rhyme and form
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Rhythm, rhyme and form",
    title="Rhythm, rhyme and form",
    slug="lit1-rhythm-rhyme-form",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Rhythm is the pattern of beats; rhyme is the echo of end sounds; form is the shape of the poem. This lesson shows how to mark stresses, label a rhyme scheme, name stanza types and explain what sound and shape add to meaning.",
    objectives=[
        "Define rhythm, rhyme, stanza and rhyme scheme and label a rhyme scheme with letters",
        "Mark stressed and unstressed syllables in a line",
        "Name common stanza types and forms (couplet, quatrain, sonnet, free verse)",
        "Explain the effect of rhythm, rhyme and form on meaning",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Rhythm", "Stress", "Metre", "Rhyme", "Rhyme scheme", "Stanza", "Couplet", "Quatrain", "Free verse", "Enjambment", "Caesura", "Refrain"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Think of a drummer. Some beats are strong and some are weak, and together they make a rhythm you can dance to. A poem also has beats. When the same sound returns at the end of lines, it is like a drum pattern coming back to the same beat. "
          "In this lesson we learn to hear and describe that pattern."),
        D("**Rhythm** is the pattern of stressed (strong) and unstressed (weak) syllables in a line. When the pattern is regular, it is called **metre**. "
          "**Rhyme** is the repetition of the same ending sound in different words, usually at the end of lines.", title="Rhythm and rhyme"),
        H("Marking stress"),
        T("A **syllable** is a unit of sound; 'rain' has one, 'candle' has two (can-dle). In a word with more than one syllable we say one part more strongly: CAN-dle. "
          "To mark a line we write / over a strong syllable and x over a weak one."),
        CODE("the night is long, the candle low,\nthe rain falls soft upon the tin;\nI count the drops and wait to know\nif morning means to let me in.",
             "text", title="Invented poem: Waiting"),
        IMG("f5lit-1-rhythm-mark.svg", alt="The line 'the night is long, the candle low' with weak and strong marks alternating over its eight syllables, and the rhyme letters A B A B beside four lines",
            caption="Figure 1. Stress marks on line 1 and the rhyme scheme A B A B."),
        T("Line 1 has eight syllables. Reading it aloud, we stress every second syllable: the NIGHT is LONG, the CAN-dle LOW. The pattern is weak-strong, repeated four times. "
          "A weak-strong pair is called an **iamb**, and a line of four iambs is called **iambic tetrameter** (check your textbook for the terms your syllabus uses). The even beat gives the poem a steady, patient feel, like someone waiting."),
        H("Rhyme scheme"),
        D("A **rhyme scheme** labels the end sounds of the lines with letters. Lines that rhyme get the same letter. Line 1 is always A. A new end sound gets the next letter.", title="Rhyme scheme"),
        EX("Worked example 1: label the rhyme scheme", intro="Give the rhyme scheme of 'Waiting'.", steps=[
            S("Line 1 ends in 'low'", text="This is A."),
            S("Line 2 ends in 'tin'", text="A new sound, so B."),
            S("Line 3 ends in 'know'", text="It rhymes with 'low', so A."),
            S("Line 4 ends in 'in'", text="It rhymes with 'tin', so B."),
            S("Answer", text="The rhyme scheme is ABAB."),
        ]),
        KEY("Words for sound and structure", "Learn these labels.", cards=[
            ["Stanza", "A group of lines in a poem, like a paragraph. A 2-line stanza is a **couplet**, 3 lines a **tercet**, 4 lines a **quatrain**."],
            ["Free verse", "A poem without a regular rhyme or metre. It still has rhythm and line breaks."],
            ["Blank verse", "Unrhymed lines in a regular beat of ten syllables (iambic pentameter)."],
            ["Sonnet", "A fourteen-line poem with a set rhyme scheme."],
            ["Refrain", "A line or phrase that repeats, like a chorus."],
            ["Enjambment and caesura", "Enjambment: a sentence runs over the line end. Caesura: a pause in the middle of a line."],
        ]),
        TIP("Do not say a poem 'has no form' just because it does not rhyme. Free verse is a form: the poet chooses where to break lines and what to put in each stanza, and that choice affects meaning."),
        EX("Worked example 2: explain the effect", intro="What does the steady rhythm and rhyme of 'Waiting' add to its meaning?", steps=[
            S("Describe the sound", text="Regular beats (iambs) and an ABAB rhyme scheme give a smooth, even, almost rocking movement."),
            S("Link to the content", text="The speaker waits through the night. The steady beat is like the patient dripping of rain and the slow passing of hours."),
            S("Look at the last line", text="'if morning means to let me in' completes the pattern with a hopeful rhyme on 'in', echoing 'tin'. The rhyme closes the stanza like a door."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Saying 'it rhymes' and stopping", "Always say what the sound does: a smooth rhyme can feel calm or playful; a broken rhyme can feel uneasy; a clash can suggest conflict."),
        MISTAKE("Labelling the scheme wrongly", "Start at A and give a new letter for each new end sound. Rhyme is by sound, not by spelling: 'low' and 'know' rhyme."),
        MISTAKE("Forcing a pattern", "In free verse, or a poem with irregular lines, do not claim a metre that is not there. Say that the rhythm is irregular and explain what that irregularity suggests, such as speech, tension or freedom."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 1 (multiple choice) asks you to name the rhyme scheme of a stanza, identify a stanza type, or choose the effect of a repeated line. Paper 2 poetry answers can use form and sound as evidence of meaning, provided you refer to the poem in your anthology.", title="Exam link"),
        QUIZ("A poem's four lines end in the words 'day', 'sea', 'play', 'tree'. The rhyme scheme is:",
             ["AABB", "ABAB", "ABBA", "ABCD"], 1,
             "'day' and 'play' rhyme (A), 'sea' and 'tree' rhyme (B), in the order A B A B.",
             hint="Mark each line with a letter in turn.",
             feedback=["AABB would need lines 1 and 2 to rhyme.", "", "ABBA would need lines 2 and 3 to rhyme.", "Here there are rhymes, so the letters repeat."]),
        QUIZ("A stanza of four lines is called a:",
             ["Couplet", "Tercet", "Quatrain", "Sonnet"], 2,
             "A quatrain has four lines.",
             hint="Quarter, quadrilateral: 'quat' relates to four.",
             feedback=["A couplet has two lines.", "A tercet has three lines.", "", "A sonnet is a whole poem of fourteen lines."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Define rhythm and rhyme.", "Rhythm is the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables. Rhyme is repetition of the same ending sound in different words."),
        PQ("The six lines of an invented stanza end in these words, in order: begins, rise, round, skies, song, sound. Give the rhyme scheme.",
           "Begins = A; rise = B; round = C; skies = B (rhymes with rise); song = D; sound = C (rhymes with round). So the scheme is ABCBDC."),
        PQ("Why might a poet choose free verse rather than a rhymed form?", "Free verse can sound like natural speech, give freedom to follow the thought, or suggest openness or disorder. The line breaks still control pace and emphasis."),
        SUM("Rhythm is the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables; metre is a regular rhythm; rhyme is the repeated end sound.",
            "A rhyme scheme labels end sounds with letters, starting at A.",
            "Stanza types: couplet (2), tercet (3), quatrain (4); a sonnet has 14 lines.",
            "Free verse has no regular rhyme or metre but is still a deliberate form.",
            "Always link sound and form to meaning."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 11. Voice and tone
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Voice and tone",
    title="Voice and tone in poetry",
    slug="lit1-voice-tone-poetry",
    minutes=22,
    summary="The voice of a poem is the speaker or persona who talks; the tone is that speaker's attitude. This lesson shows how to identify the speaker, the person addressed, the tone and any shift in tone, with a close reading of an invented poem.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish the poet from the speaker (persona) of a poem",
        "Identify who is addressed and the situation",
        "Name the tone of a poem and support it from diction, imagery and sound",
        "Spot and explain a shift in tone",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Speaker", "Persona", "Addressee", "Voice", "Tone", "Shift in tone", "Attitude", "Dramatic monologue"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Imagine hearing only one side of a phone call. You still learn a lot: from the words, the pauses and the way the voice rises or falls, you can tell whether the caller is angry, shy or sad. "
          "A poem is a voice on the page. To read it well you must hear who is talking and how they feel."),
        D("The **speaker** (or **persona**) is the voice that speaks in the poem. The speaker may be the poet or an invented character: a child, a market woman, an old man or even an object. "
          "The **addressee** is the person or thing the speaker talks to.", title="Speaker and addressee"),
        T("Do not assume the speaker is the poet. When a poem says 'I', ask: who is this 'I'? What are they like? Where are they? When you write, use 'the speaker' or 'the persona' unless your teacher has given you reason to speak of the poet."),
        D("**Tone** is the speaker's attitude towards the subject and the listener, shown through word choice, images, sound and rhythm. It may be joyful, bitter, nostalgic, ironic, respectful, defiant, tender and so on.", title="Tone"),
        T("Here is an invented poem. It is not from any anthology."),
        CODE("You ask me, cousin, why I do not smile\nnow that I wear the city's polished shoes.\nI smile. I smile at every office door.\nIt is the smile that stays behind that hurts.\n\nBut come the dry season, take the road,\nand tell my mother that the shoes are fine.",
             "text", title="Invented poem: The Returnee"),
        EX("Worked example 1: voice and situation", intro="Who speaks in 'The Returnee', to whom, and in what situation?", steps=[
            S("Speaker", text="A young person who has moved to the city and works in offices; the persona, not necessarily the poet."),
            S("Addressee", text="A cousin who has stayed at home and asked why the speaker looks unhappy."),
            S("Situation", text="The speaker answers the question and then, in the last two lines, sends a message to the mother."),
            S("Answer", text="A city worker replies to a cousin at home, and then asks the cousin to carry a reassuring message to the mother."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: tone and shift", intro="Describe the tone of 'The Returnee' and explain any shift.", steps=[
            S("First stanza", text="The words 'I smile' are repeated, with a flat, almost sarcastic ring. 'Polished shoes' hints at pride in appearance but also at pretence. 'That hurts' reveals real pain. The tone is guarded, bitter-sad."),
            S("Second stanza", text="'But come the dry season, take the road' turns towards home. The tone softens: it becomes tender and protective."),
            S("Look at the last line", text="'tell my mother that the shoes are fine' hides his troubles from her. The tone is loving but sad."),
            S("Answer", text="The tone moves from guarded bitterness in the first stanza to tenderness and quiet sorrow in the second. The shift at 'But' marks the change."),
        ]),
        KEY("Clues to tone", "Look at:", cards=[
            ["Diction", "'hurts', 'polished', 'smile' repeated: guarded and sad."],
            ["Sound and rhythm", "Short, flat sentences ('I smile.') sound controlled and clipped."],
            ["Imagery", "The polished shoes, the office door: outward success covering inner hurt."],
            ["Structure", "A turning word such as 'But' or 'Yet' often marks a shift in tone."],
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Naming the tone with one weak word", "'Sad' is not enough. Use a precise word (bitter, nostalgic, defiant, tender, mocking, proud) and prove it from the poem."),
        MISTAKE("Treating the speaker as the poet", "A poem may speak in the voice of someone quite different from the poet. Say 'the speaker' unless the question says otherwise."),
        MISTAKE("Ignoring the shift", "Many poems change tone. Look for turning words like 'but' or 'yet', for a change of image, or for a change in the length of lines."),
        MISTAKE("Personal opinion instead of the text", "The Board's report says that candidates lost marks by bringing in their own experience. Write about the speaker's tone as shown by words in the poem, not about how you would feel in the same situation."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 1 (multiple choice) often asks for the tone of a short poem or the attitude of the speaker, or which word shows the tone. Paper 2 Section C may ask how the poet's choice of voice or tone helps convey the message of a poem in your anthology.", title="Exam link"),
        QUIZ("In a poem, the 'persona' is:",
             ["The poet's real name", "The voice that speaks in the poem", "The title", "The rhyme scheme"], 1,
             "The persona is the voice or character who speaks, who may differ from the poet.",
             hint="It comes from a Latin word for a mask worn by an actor.",
             feedback=["The poet's name is not the persona.", "", "The title names the poem.", "The rhyme scheme is part of the form."]),
        QUIZ("What does the word 'But' at the start of the second stanza of 'The Returnee' suggest?",
             ["The tone is unchanged", "A shift in direction and tone", "A rhyme", "A new speaker"], 1,
             "A turning word signals a change: from guarded bitterness to tenderness.",
             hint="What does 'But' do in a sentence?",
             feedback=["'But' marks a contrast, so something changes.", "", "'But' is not a rhyme here.", "The speaker is the same person."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Explain the difference between the poet and the speaker of a poem.", "The poet writes the poem. The speaker (persona) is the voice inside it, who may be quite different from the poet."),
        PQ("Give three words that could describe tone and use each to describe a different imaginary poem.",
           "For example: bitter (a poem about being cheated), tender (a poem about a mother), playful (a poem about children at play)."),
        PQ("In 'The Returnee', explain how the repeated 'I smile' helps to show the speaker's feelings.",
           "The repetition and the short, flat sentence make the smile sound forced and automatic, so the reader senses the speaker hides pain behind a polite surface."),
        SUM("The speaker or persona is the voice in the poem and may differ from the poet.",
            "The addressee is the listener, who may be a person, a place or the reader.",
            "Tone is the speaker's attitude and is shown by diction, imagery, sound and structure.",
            "Look for shifts in tone at turning words such as 'but' and 'yet'.",
            "Name tone precisely and prove it from the words of the poem."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 12. Themes
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Themes in the prescribed poems",
    title="Themes in poetry: finding them and writing about them",
    slug="lit1-poetry-themes",
    minutes=25,
    summary="A theme is the main idea a poem explores. This lesson shows how to move from subject to theme, build a theme chart for your own anthology, and write a full answer on a poem's theme using only the text. It does not describe any prescribed poem: use your own anthology.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish the subject of a poem from its theme",
        "Move from images and tone to a clear theme statement",
        "Build a theme chart for the poems in your own anthology",
        "Write a structured answer about the theme of a poem using evidence from the text only",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Theme", "Subject", "Message", "Theme statement", "Theme chart", "Evidence", "Compare and contrast", "Staying with the text"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Suppose two friends watch the same film. One says 'It is about a boy who leaves home.' The other says 'It shows how leaving home teaches a person to value family.' The first friend has named the **subject**. The second has stated a **theme**. In poetry we need both."),
        D("The **subject** is what the poem is about: its topic or situation. The **theme** is the central idea or message the poem explores about that subject.", title="Subject and theme"),
        T("A good theme statement is a full sentence. It says something about the subject. Not 'education', but 'education gives hope to people who have little'. Not 'death', but 'grief is shared by a whole community'."),
        H("From text to theme"),
        T("You cannot find a theme by guessing. You build it from the poem step by step."),
        KEY("Four steps to a theme", "Do them in this order.", cards=[
            ["1. Subject", "What is the poem about? Say it in plain words."],
            ["2. Evidence", "Which images, words, tone and structure stand out?"],
            ["3. Attitude", "What does the speaker feel or want us to feel about the subject?"],
            ["4. Theme statement", "Combine them into one sentence: subject plus message."],
        ]),
        T("Here is an invented poem. It is not from any anthology."),
        CODE("Her bag is made from a rice sack,\nstitched by her mother's uneven hand.\nAt the roadside she lifts it high\nabove the muddy water, as if it held\nthe whole bright country inside.",
             "text", title="Invented poem: The Schoolbag"),
        EX("Worked example 1: from text to theme", intro="State the theme of 'The Schoolbag'.", steps=[
            S("Subject", text="A girl crossing a muddy road with a homemade school bag."),
            S("Evidence", text="The bag is a rice sack, 'stitched by her mother's uneven hand', so it is poor but made with love. She lifts it 'high above the muddy water' to protect it. 'The whole bright country' suggests hopes for the future."),
            S("Attitude", text="Admiring and tender. The speaker honours the family's sacrifice."),
            S("Theme statement", text="Education is precious to those who have little, and families sacrifice and hope to protect it."),
        ]),
        H("Building a theme chart for your anthology"),
        T("In your own anthology, make a chart like the one below for each poem. Fill it in from the poem itself. The chart makes it easy to answer a question on a named poem and to compare poems."),
        CODE("Poem title:\nSubject (plain words):\nSpeaker and addressee:\nKey images or symbols (with line quotes):\nTone (and any shift):\nForm and sound (stanzas, rhyme, rhythm):\nTheme statement (one sentence):\nOne short quotation I can use:",
             "text", title="Theme chart (copy into your notebook)"),
        T("Poems in many anthologies, African and others, return to certain broad themes: identity and belonging, family and community, nature and the land, love, death and mourning, change and the past, injustice, hope and resilience. "
          "Check which of these your own anthology really explores, and which poem explores which. Do not assume: base it on the text."),
        D("To **compare** poems, say how they are alike and how they differ in subject, theme, tone and form. To **contrast** is to focus on the differences.", title="Compare and contrast"),
        H("Writing the answer"),
        KEY("A structure for a poem question", "Use it for any named poem.", cards=[
            ["Introduction", "Name the poem, its speaker and subject, and state the theme in one sentence."],
            ["Body", "Take the poem in order, stanza by stanza. Use point, evidence, explanation. Link images, tone and form to the theme."],
            ["Conclusion", "Restate the theme and the effect on the reader. Do not add new points."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a model answer", intro="Question (invented): How does the poet show the value of education in 'The Schoolbag'?", steps=[
            S("Introduction", text="In 'The Schoolbag', an observer describes a girl protecting her homemade bag. The poem shows that education is valued deeply by those who have little."),
            S("Body 1", text="The bag is made 'from a rice sack, stitched by her mother's uneven hand'. The humble material shows poverty, and the mother's stitching shows love and sacrifice."),
            S("Body 2", text="The girl lifts the bag 'high above the muddy water'. The action shows she cares more for the bag than for her own comfort."),
            S("Body 3", text="The final image says the bag holds 'the whole bright country'. It makes learning the key to the future."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The poem presents education as a precious hope shared by the girl and her family."),
        ]),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Bringing in personal experience", "The Board's report on the new anthology says that on a named poem many candidates gave their own thoughts and experiences on the continent instead of an account of the poem. Do not do this. Every point must come from the poem."),
        MISTAKE("Naming a subject instead of a theme", "'The poem is about poverty' is a subject. 'The poem shows that dignity can survive poverty' is a theme."),
        MISTAKE("Describing poems you have not read", "Write only about poems you have actually studied from your own copy. Do not describe or guess the content of a poem that you have not read."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up poems", "In another section the report notes that candidates mixed up material between questions, and the same risk applies to poems. Keep a separate chart for each poem and check it before writing."),
        TIP("Exam link: Paper 1 (multiple choice) asks for the theme or message of a short poem or passage. Paper 2 Section C has two poetry questions. The Board's report says one popular question was familiar in poem and theme, while the other was attempted by few and poorly. Prepare every poem in your anthology so you can choose the question you can answer best.", title="Exam link"),
        QUIZ("Which of these is a THEME rather than a subject?",
             ["Water", "A girl crossing a road", "Hardship can be borne with quiet dignity", "A rice sack"], 2,
             "A theme is a full statement of an idea; the others are topics or objects.",
             hint="A theme says something about the subject.",
             feedback=["'Water' names a topic.", "This is a description of the situation.", "", "A rice sack is an object."]),
        QUIZ("What should a poetry answer be based on?",
             ["Your own experience of the subject", "The words and ideas in the poem", "What your friends think", "The poet's life story"], 1,
             "A literature answer stays with the text and uses the poem's words as evidence.",
             hint="What did the Board warn against?",
             feedback=["The Board warns against this.", "", "Opinions of others are not evidence from the poem.", "The report advises focusing on the text."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Explain the difference between the subject and the theme of a poem, using 'The Schoolbag' as an example.",
           "The subject is a girl crossing a muddy road with a homemade school bag. The theme is that education is precious to those who have little."),
        PQ("List the four steps for finding the theme of a poem.", "Subject, evidence (images, words, tone), attitude, theme statement."),
        PQ("Write a theme statement for 'The Calabash' (the invented poem in the imagery lesson).",
           "Objects that hold family memory continue to be valued even after they lose their use. (Other answers are acceptable if supported by the poem.)"),
        PQ("Copy the theme chart and fill it for one poem from your own anthology.",
           "Check your answer with your teacher: the subject, tone, images and theme statement must all come from the poem itself."),
        SUM("The subject is what the poem is about; the theme is the idea it explores about that subject.",
            "Build a theme from evidence: subject, images and words, attitude, theme statement.",
            "Keep a theme chart for each poem in your own anthology.",
            "A good answer has an introduction with the theme, a body that moves through the poem, and a conclusion.",
            "Stay with the text: no personal experience, no guessing about poems you have not read."),
    ],
))
